---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import Figure from '../components/Figure.astro';
import Math from '../components/Math.astro';
import { site } from '../data/site';

const softwareSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'SoftwareSourceCode',
  name: 'SMVac',
  description:
    'A modular C++/Python framework for precision studies of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model: NNLO matching, 3-loop RG evolution, the one-loop Coleman–Weinberg effective potential, and semiclassical vacuum decay via an RG-improved Fubini–Lipatov calculation.',
  codeRepository: site.smvac,
  author: { '@type': 'Person', name: site.name, url: site.url },
  programmingLanguage: ['C++', 'Python'],
  dateModified: '2026-07-18',
};
---
<Base
  title="SMVac · electroweak vacuum stability"
  description="SMVac: a C++/Python framework for precision studies of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model. 3-loop RG evolution, NNLO matching, the one-loop Coleman–Weinberg potential, and semiclassical vacuum decay via an RG-improved Fubini–Lipatov calculation, with phase diagrams in the (Mh, Mt) plane."
  jsonLd={[softwareSchema]}
>
  <div class="container">
    <header class="page-head reveal">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research software · C++ / Python</p>
      <h1>SMVac</h1>
      <p class="lede" style="max-width: 42rem;">
        Precision computation of electroweak vacuum stability in the Standard Model.
      </p>
      <p class="meta">
        <strong style="color: var(--ink); font-weight: 600;">Naman Goyal</strong> · May 2024 – May 2026 ·
        Mentored by Prof. Rick Sandepan Gupta (TIFR) ·
        <a href={site.smvac}>github.com/namangoyal31/SMVac</a>
      </p>
    </header>

    <section class="section prose" id="overview" aria-labelledby="overview">
      <h2 id="overview" style="margin-top: 1rem;"><span class="secno">§ 01</span> Overview</h2>
      <p>
        The Standard Model of particle physics appears, from its measured parameters, to live on a
        knife's edge. At field values far beyond any collider, the effective Higgs potential turns
        over: the quartic coupling <Math tex={'\\lambda(\\mu)'} />, run up in energy, becomes negative
        at scales of order <Math tex={'10^{10}\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />. The electroweak vacuum in which
        we live is then not the absolute minimum of the theory but a <em>metastable</em> one,
        destined in principle to decay by quantum tunneling. Whether that decay can ever matter is
        controlled by the semiclassical bounce action <Math tex={'S'} />: the vacuum survives
        cosmological timescales provided <Math tex={'S'} /> is large enough. The measured Higgs and
        top masses place our universe uncomfortably close to the boundary where this guarantee fails.
      </p>
      <p>
        SMVac is the computational framework I developed to study this problem quantitatively. It
        combines precision renormalization group evolution with the semiclassical
        Fubini–Lipatov (conformal) ansatz: rather than assuming a constant quartic coupling, the
        code numerically integrates the full running, one-loop effective potential over the
        analytical conformal field profile, yielding an <strong>RG-improved decay action</strong>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="problem" aria-labelledby="problem-heading">
      <h2 id="problem-heading"><span class="secno">§ 02</span> The problem</h2>
      <p>
        Why the <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> plane? The top quark drives <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> down
        through the RG flow, the Higgs mass pushes it up; the stability verdict is therefore a curve
        in the Higgs–top mass plane, and the experimental point
        <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t) \\approx (125.1, 173.1)\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> sits close to it.
        Classic analyses (Buttazzo et al. 2013) mapped this boundary with multi-loop precision.
      </p>
      <p>
        The standard analytical shortcut evaluates the decay rate in the strict Fubini–Lipatov
        limit, where the potential is assumed purely quartic,
        <Math tex={'V \\approx \\tfrac{1}{4}\\lambda\\,\\phi^4'} /> with <em>constant</em>
        <Math tex={'\\lambda < 0'} /> at high scales. But near the metastability boundary the running
        of the couplings is precisely what matters. The regime where the shortcut is most tempting
        is exactly the regime where it is least reliable, and SMVac quantifies this.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="method" aria-labelledby="method-heading">
      <h2 id="method-heading"><span class="secno">§ 03</span> Method</h2>
      <p>The computational pipeline, as implemented in the repository:</p>
      <ol class="pipeline">
        <li>Input <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> and <Math tex={'\\alpha_s(M_Z)'} /></li>
        <li>NNLO matching at the top scale <Math tex={'M_t'} /></li>
        <li>3-loop RG evolution to the Planck scale (RK4)</li>
        <li>Locate <Math tex={'\\lambda_{\\min}'} /> and the instability scale <Math tex={'\\mu_{\\rm inst}'} /></li>
        <li>RG-improved Fubini–Lipatov action over the conformal profile</li>
        <li>Optimize the profile scale <Math tex={'R'} /> (Golden Section Search)</li>
        <li>Compare <Math tex={'S'} /> with the universe-age threshold</li>
        <li>Repeat over an <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> grid → phase diagram</li>
      </ol>

      <h3>Effective potential</h3>
      <p>
        The tree-level potential is improved by the running coupling, with one-loop
        Coleman–Weinberg corrections from the top, <Math tex={'W'} />, and <Math tex={'Z'} /> fields:
      </p>
      <Math
        block
        tex={'V_{\\rm eff}(\\phi) \\;\\approx\\; \\tfrac{1}{4}\\,\\lambda_{\\rm eff}(\\mu)\\,\\phi^{4} \\;+\\; \\Delta V_{\\rm 1\\text{-}loop}(\\phi), \\qquad \\mu \\sim \\phi.'}
      />

      <h3>Renormalization group evolution</h3>
      <p>
        The couplings <Math tex={'(g_1, g_2, g_3, y_t, \\lambda)'} />, with
        <Math tex={'y_b, y_\\tau'} /> subleading, are evolved with their three-loop beta functions
        by fixed-step RK4 integration in <Math tex={'t = 2\\ln\\mu'} />, from the matching scale
        <Math tex={'M_t'} /> to the Planck scale:
      </p>
      <Math block tex={'\\frac{d g_i}{d \\ln \\mu} \\;=\\; \\beta_{g_i}^{\\rm (3\\text{-}loop)}.'} />

      <h3>Semiclassical decay action</h3>
      <p>
        SMVac retains the analytical Fubini–Lipatov field profile,
        <Math tex={"\\phi_{\\rm FL}(r) = \\sqrt{2/|\\lambda_R|}\\; 2R^{2}/(r^{2}+R^{2})"} />, but
        evaluates the Euclidean action honestly along it: the kinetic term is closed-form in the
        conformal limit, while the potential term is integrated numerically over the full running
        <Math tex={'V_{\\rm eff}'} /> (composite Simpson's rule, <Math tex={'N = 2048'} /> points),
        and the profile radius <Math tex={'R'} /> is optimized by Golden Section Search to tolerance
        <Math tex={'10^{-13}'} />:
      </p>
      <Math
        block
        tex={'S \\;=\\; \\underbrace{\\frac{16\\pi^{2}}{3\\,|\\lambda_R|}}_{\\rm closed\\ form} \\;+\\; \\underbrace{2\\pi^{2}\\!\\int_0^{\\infty}\\! dr\\; r^{3}\\,\\bigl[V_{\\rm eff}(\\phi_{\\rm FL}) - V_{\\rm eff}(0)\\bigr]}_{\\rm numerical}, \\qquad \\frac{\\Gamma}{V} \\sim A\\,e^{-S}.'}
      />
      <p>
        A point of the plane is classified <em>stable</em> if <Math tex={'\\lambda'} /> never turns
        negative, <em>unstable</em> if <Math tex={'S'} /> falls below the universe-age threshold
        <Math tex={'S_{\\rm th} \\approx 4\\ln(T_U\\,\\mu_{\\rm inst})'} />, and
        <em>metastable</em> otherwise. A perturbativity guard flags regions where couplings exceed
        <Math tex={'4\\pi'} />.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="results" aria-labelledby="results-heading">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="results-heading" style="margin-top: 0;"><span class="secno">§ 04</span> Results</h2>
        <p>
          Sweeps of roughly <Math tex={'10^{6}'} /> grid points across the
          <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t)'} /> plane, parallelized with OpenMP and a 12-process Python
          pipeline, produce the stability phase diagram with constant-action contours. The physical
          point (star) sits in the metastable region, close to the boundary, in agreement with the
          literature.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src="/figures/smvac-phase-diagram.png"
        alt="Phase diagram of the Standard Model vacuum across the Higgs–top mass plane with constant-action contours. Stable region in green, metastable in yellow, unstable in red, non-perturbative in black; the experimental point (125.1, 173.1) GeV is marked with a star inside the metastable region."
        width={1600}
        height={1302}
        label="Fig. 1"
        caption="Phase diagram of the Standard Model vacuum across the (M_h, M_t) plane with constant-action contours of the RG-improved Fubini–Lipatov action. The experimental point (M_h, M_t) = (125.1, 173.1) GeV (star) lies in the metastable region, near the stability boundary. Figure from the SMVac repository."
      />
      <Figure
        src="/figures/smvac-near-experimental-point.png"
        alt="Close-up of the stability phase diagram near the experimental point: the boundary between stable (green) and metastable (yellow) regions runs just below the starred experimental point at (125.1, 173.1) GeV."
        width={1600}
        height={1297}
        label="Fig. 2"
        caption="Close-up around the physical point: the absolute-stability boundary (green/yellow) runs just below (125.1, 173.1) GeV, which is why the vacuum-stability problem is so sensitive to precision. Figure from the SMVac repository."
      />
    </section>

    <section class="section" id="analytical-vs-rg-improved" aria-labelledby="avsi-heading">
      <div class="prose">
        <h2 id="avsi-heading" style="margin-top: 0;"><span class="secno">§ 05</span> Analytical vs. RG-improved</h2>
        <p>
          The RG-improved action can be benchmarked against the strict analytical conformal limit
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm approx} = 8\\pi^2 / (3|\\lambda_{\\min}|)'} />. Deep in the metastable
          region the two agree to about <strong>0.01%</strong>, since the conformal limit holds when
          the coupling is large and slowly varying. Near the metastability boundary, however, they
          disagree at the <strong>tens-of-percent</strong> level (e.g. 32.1% at
          <Math tex={'(M_h, M_t) = (134.75, 176.5)\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />), and the disagreement
          misclassifies points right at the boundary: precisely the scientifically interesting
          strip of the phase diagram.
        </p>
      </div>
      <Figure
        src="/figures/smvac-analytical-vs-rgimproved.png"
        alt="Overlay of the stability classification from the analytical conformal approximation and the RG-improved calculation. Blue marks the region of disagreement, concentrated in a band along the metastability boundary and at low Higgs masses."
        width={1600}
        height={1303}
        label="Fig. 3"
        caption="Where the strict analytical approximation (constant-coupling conformal limit) misclassifies the vacuum relative to the RG-improved calculation (blue): concentrated along the metastability boundary. Figure from the SMVac repository."
      />
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="validation" aria-labelledby="validation-heading">
      <h2 id="validation-heading"><span class="secno">§ 06</span> Validation</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Analytical limit.</strong> The numerical machinery reproduces
          <Math tex={'S_{\\rm approx} = 8\\pi^2/(3|\\lambda_{\\min}|)'} /> in the deep-metastability
          regime to about <Math tex={'0.01\\%'} /> (verified against the repository's reference data).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Literature boundary.</strong> The computed stability boundary at
          <Math tex={'M_h \\approx 125\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} /> lies at
          <Math tex={'M_t \\approx 171\\text{–}173\\,\\mathrm{GeV}'} />, consistent with the NNLO
          analysis of Buttazzo et al. (2013).
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Reference data.</strong> The repository ships benchmark outputs
          (<code>reference/v1.0/</code>) for the RGE, potential, bounce action, and phase scans,
          alongside CI that checks compilation.
        </li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="limitations" aria-labelledby="limitations-heading">
      <h2 id="limitations-heading"><span class="secno">§ 07</span> Limitations</h2>
      <p>Stated plainly, because they bound what the results mean:</p>
      <div class="callout">
        <ul>
          <li>
            <strong>No exact bounce solver.</strong> SMVac does not integrate the Euclidean equation
            of motion <Math tex={"\\phi'' + \\tfrac{3}{r}\\phi' = dV/d\\phi"} />. The kinetic term is
            evaluated in the constant-&lambda; conformal limit; only the potential term is fully
            numerical. The result is an RG-improved <em>semi-analytical</em> action, not an exact
            bounce solution.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>One-loop potential.</strong> The effective potential includes one-loop
            Coleman–Weinberg corrections over the tree-level running; the RG flow itself is
            three-loop.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Fixed-step integration.</strong> The production RGE integrator uses a fixed RK4
            step; an adaptive stepper exists in the codebase but is not used in the main pipeline.
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Research software.</strong> Sweep parameters are hardcoded in the Python
            wrappers and the top-level solver has no formal build system (the modularized
            <code>SMVacuumDecay/</code> library adds CMake-oriented structure and tests).
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="code" aria-labelledby="code-heading">
      <h2 id="code-heading"><span class="secno">§ 08</span> Code</h2>
      <p>
        The full source, documentation, reference data, and reproducibility notes are on GitHub.
        The main solvers compile directly with a C++17 compiler and OpenMP:
      </p>
      <pre class="codeblock"><code>git clone https://github.com/namangoyal31/SMVac.git
cd SMVac
g++ -std=c++17 -O3 -fopenmp apps/solver_numerical.cpp -o apps/generate_phase_diagram.exe
python scripts/run_numerical.py          # (M_h, M_t) sweep → data/
python scripts/plot_numerical_contours.py # data/ → figures/</code></pre>
      <p style="margin-top: 1.2rem;">
        If you use SMVac, please cite it via the repository's
        <a href="https://github.com/namangoyal31/SMVac/blob/main/CITATION.cff">CITATION.cff</a>.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section class="section prose" id="future" aria-labelledby="future-heading">
      <h2 id="future-heading"><span class="secno">§ 09</span> Future work</h2>
      <p>
        Planned, as recorded in the repository: full numerical bounce solutions through
        boundary-value methods (shooting or relaxation), a formal CMake build, a proper C++ testing
        framework for the quadrature tolerances, and configurable sweep parameters.
      </p>
    </section>
  </div>
</Base>
